(window.DVR_PAPERS=window.DVR_PAPERS||{})["da-easy-2026-008"]={"v":1,"id":"da-easy-2026-008","title":"Basic Level Test-4 Full Syllabus GATE 2026 DA","sourceTitle":"Basic Level Test-4 Full Syllabus GATE 2026 DA","branch":"DATA SCIENCE AND ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE","code":"DA","provider":"DVRUO EASY","providerKey":"easy","series":"GATE 2026 Online Test Series - Data Science and AI","seriesId":"da-easy-2026","num":8,"category":"basic","year":"2026","format":"A","durationSec":10800,"info":{"meta":"Package: GATE 2026 + GATE 2025 Online Test Series - Data Science and AI • Questions: 65","package":"GATE 2026 + GATE 2025 Online Test Series - Data Science and AI"},"source":"DATA SCIENCE AND ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE/MADE EASY/GATE_2026_+_GATE_2025_Online_Test_Series_Data_Science_and_AI/016_Basic Level Test-4 Full Syllabus GATE 2026 DA.html","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>A book consists of 30 pages, 25 lines on each page and 35 characters on each line. If this content is written in another notebook consisting of 30 lines and 28 characters per line, then the required no. of pages will be how much percent greater than the previous pages?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"4.16%"},{"key":"B","html":"5%"},{"key":"C","html":"6.66%"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/04/04addb0d3bb3f40625cf.png\" /></div>"},{"n":2,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The rate of increase of the price of sugar is observed to be two percent more than the inflation rate expressed in percentage. The price of sugar on January 1, 2004 is <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/3f/3f93f1c822cb38088a0b.png\" /> per kg. The inflation rates for the years 2004 and 2005 are expected to be 8% each. The expected price of sugar on January 1, 2006 would be :</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/77/77d7d6f4de35cb57d249.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/59/59d0f66f3a96d9b1e359.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e1/e19a40f23f6c964d9427.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/bb/bb03488fe23e7d541f62.png\" />"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ca/ca1627876f8143b5585f.png\" /></div>"},{"n":3,"type":"MSQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The following question is based on the diagram given below:<br />\n1. The rectangle represents government employees.<br />\n2. The triangle represents urban people.<br />\n3. The circle represents graduates.<br />\n4. The square represents clerks.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is true?</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/39/39f936676d214453110f.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"All government employees are clerks."},{"key":"B","html":"Some government employees are graduates as well as clerks."},{"key":"C","html":"All government employees are graduates."},{"key":"D","html":"All clerks are government employees but not graduates."}],"answer":["A","B","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, A, C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nThe above cases may be considered as under:<br />\nFor statement (a) to be true, the rectangle should lie inside the square. This is not true. Hence, (a) is false.<br />\nFor statement (b) to be true, there should be a region common to the rectangle, circle and the<br />\nsquare. Such a region is 6. Hence, (b) is true.<br />\nFurther, for statement (c) to be true, the rectangle should lie inside the circle. So, (c) is false.<br />\nFor statement (d) to be true, square should lie wholly inside the rectangle, with no region common<br />\nto the circle. This is not true. So, (d) is false.</p>"},{"n":4,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A square is inscribed in a circle and then another circle is inscribed in the square. Another square is inscribed in the circle. Finally, a circle is inscribed in the innermost square. Thus, there are 3 circles and 2 squares as shown in the figure. The radius of the outer-most circle is <em>R</em>.<br />\nWhat is the ratio of sum of circumferences of all the circles to the sum of perimeters of all the squares?</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/99/99f05d148937f1075001.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/06/064f1704381a9dc69b5a.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/cc/cc205699d70906b08347.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f8/f838f32a6349d5df6548.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b4/b414c5fc6319483f9c44.png\" />"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/16/16f0af445cab04b90d31.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/34/3423416468bb89809804.png\" /></div>"},{"n":5,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Which one of the following is the synonym of the word written in capital letter?<br />\nIRREVERENCE</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"disrespect"},{"key":"B","html":"cruelty"},{"key":"C","html":"unkindness"},{"key":"D","html":"invalidity"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\nIrreverence means a lack of respect for the people or things which generally are taken seriously.</p>"},{"n":6,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Read the passage carefully and answer the following question.<br />\nChild psychology is certainly not a strong point with most Indian schools; why else would they inflict a double trauma on a student performing badly in the pre-boards by banning her from taking the board exams. Often with fatal results as evidenced by reports of student suicides in the run-up to the boards. Now the Central Board of Secondary Education (CBSE) has stepped in and put the brakes on this discriminatory practice, ruling that no student can be barred from the Boards without prior clearance from the CBSE. This is good news for parents and students, many of whom have had to live with the threat of performance-linked debarment. While the school’s logic is that in order to attract talented students, they need to maintain their performance records at high levels. Chances are that a student faring poorly at the pre-boards will replicate at the boards is faulty. Chances are that the student will be spurred to work doubly hard. On the other hand, the threat of debarment, will almost certainly impact adversely on his/her performance. Of course, linking pre-boards to the boards is only one of the problems with our school system.<br />\nWhich is the good news for parents, according to the passage?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"School will take the responsibility of preparing students for the board."},{"key":"B","html":"School will provide study facilities to the poor student."},{"key":"C","html":"Schools will enforce discipline to ensure higher attendance of students."},{"key":"D","html":"No students can be barred from the Boards without prior clearance from the CBSE."}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(d)<br />\nFrom the above passage, it is apparent that the good news for parents is that no students can be<br />\nbarred from the Boards without prior clearance from the CBSE.</p>"},{"n":7,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The sum of n terms of two arithmetic series are in the ratio of  <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/f8/f8f2757e9abe1dad3a4b.png\" /> Find the ratio of their 11<sup>th </sup>terms:</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"4 : 3"},{"key":"B","html":"5 : 4"},{"key":"C","html":"7 : 4"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d8/d8133771b44128512c6a.png\" /></div>"},{"n":8,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>A child is looking for his father. He went 90 m in the east before turning to his right. He went 20 m before turning to his right again to look for his father at his uncle’s place 30 m from this point. His father was not there. From there, he went 100 metres to his north before meeting his father in a street. How far did the son meet his father from the starting point?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"80 metres"},{"key":"B","html":"100 metres"},{"key":"C","html":"140 metres"},{"key":"D","html":"260 metres"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/9b/9bb3e91b476c8ca11db5.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/bb/bb3dac69d2949210c1d0.png\" /></div>"},{"n":9,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>400 student took the MOCK GATE exam in Lucknow. 60% of the boys and 80% of the girls cleared the cut-off in the examination. If the total percentage of students qualifying is 65%, then</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"100 girls appeared in the exam"},{"key":"B","html":"300 girls appeared in the exam"},{"key":"C","html":"200 boys appeared in the exam"},{"key":"D","html":"100 boys appeared in the exam"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div>\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/18/185579b3a050ada840a0.png\" /></div>\n</div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":10,"type":"MCQ","section":"General Aptitude","tags":"General Aptitude • ELECTRICAL ENGINEERING • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>For the mirror represented by a line MN, choose the correct mirror-image of the figure, from amongst the four alternatives (a), (b), (c) and (d) given along with it.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/09/09ad5c03ef2618092a9a.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b4/b4aee0e1817ad6a5a822.png\" /></div>"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/8a/8a63321459868b9c1645.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b3/b3ac8a66ef31c1bad8cd.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b6/b6c0a8d9c1926aec7498.png\" />"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>"},{"n":11,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Let <em>X</em> be a continuous random variable with cumulative function <em>F<sub>X</sub></em>(<em>x</em>). Another random variable <em>Y</em> is defined as, <em>Y</em> = <em>F<sub>X</sub></em>(<em>x</em>). The mean of Y will be ________?</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[0.5,0.5],"answerText":"0.5","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>0.5 [0.5 - 0.5]</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c9/c906479b60454ec08bdc.png\" /></div>"},{"n":12,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Let <em>P</em> be the projection matrix onto the column space of <em>A</em>. For any vector y, which subspace does <em>y</em> – <em>Py</em> belong to?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Null space of <em>A</em>"},{"key":"B","html":"Row space of <em>A</em>"},{"key":"C","html":"Column space of <em>A<sup>T</sup></em>"},{"key":"D","html":"Left null space of <em>A</em>"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/79/792e72f37efa61443910.png\" /></div>"},{"n":13,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following Python code:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f2/f2286e5d0820e46834ed.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>What is the output of the above code?</div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[10,10],"answerText":"10","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>10</p>"},{"n":14,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>The number of children in a family of a region may be 0, 1 or 2 with probability 0.2, 0.3 and 0.5 respectively. The probability of each child being a Boy or Girl is 0.5, then find the probability that a family has no boy.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"0.520"},{"key":"B","html":"0.515"},{"key":"C","html":"0.517"},{"key":"D","html":"0.527"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/fb/fbf653cf54ffea415375.png\" /></div>"},{"n":15,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Match the following algorithms with their respective time complexities for the given problem.<br />\n[Worst case]<br />\n“Find k largest elements in an unsorted array given an array of n distinct elements”.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/da/da78b78bf6df0f980386.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"1 - Q, 2 - R, 3 - P"},{"key":"B","html":"1 - R, 2 - Q, 3 - P"},{"key":"C","html":"1 - Q, 2 - P, 3 - R"},{"key":"D","html":"1 - R, 2 - P, 3 - Q"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/88/88324e94ef4b450a3e1c.png\" /></div>"},{"n":16,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p><img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/4c/4c07cae69ba60a99679c.png\" /></p>\n\n<p>Find the smallest value of <em>k</em> that makes <em>f</em> convex.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/0e/0ed83d39c4424b49bd97.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c2/c28690148ebbddb36ec4.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/8d/8d39a90405157246b197.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/61/6104876bf980b0a04074.png\" />"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/63/638624dc606b9bda4688.png\" /></div>"},{"n":17,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following statements:<br />\n<strong><em>S</em><sub>1</sub> :</strong> Lasso regression cannot overcome severe multi-collinearity.<br />\n<strong><em>S</em><sub>2</sub> :</strong> Logistic regression cannot overcome severe multi-collinearity.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>1</sub>"},{"key":"B","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"C","html":"Both <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> nor <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\n&bull; Lasso regression cannot overcome severe multi-collinearity. Lasso applies an L1 penalty that<br />\ncan reduce or eliminate some correlated predictors, but under severe multi-collinearity, it<br />\ndoes not completely resolve the instability. So, S1 is correct.<br />\n&bull; Logistic regression can overcome severe multi-collinearity. Logistic regression does not include<br />\nany penalty term by default and therefore suffers from multi-collinearity just like linear<br />\nregression. It cannot overcome the problem. So, S2 is incorrect.</p>"},{"n":18,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following statements about two-player games:<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : In a zero-sum two-player game, the total payoff of the two players is always zero.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : In every zero-sum two-player game, one player must always win and the other must lose.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>1</sub>"},{"key":"B","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"C","html":"Both <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> nor <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\n&bull; A zero-sum game is defined such that one player&rsquo;s gain is exactly equal to the other player&rsquo;s loss. This means that if we add the payoffs of both players, the total is always zero. This property holds for all zero-sum two-player games. So, <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> is correct.<br />\n&bull; Although the game is zero-sum, it does not require that one player must always win and the other must lose. Some zero-sum games can end in a tie, where neither player wins. So, <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>&nbsp;is incorrect.</p>"},{"n":19,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following relation:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7f/7fc67477d8181b11636d.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Customers who buy from atleast one stare."},{"key":"B","html":"Customers who buy from atleast two stares."},{"key":"C","html":"Customers who buy from all stares."},{"key":"D","html":"Customers who do not buy anything at all."}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/32/3281aaa403a39a4cea99.png\" /></div>"},{"n":20,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>The following Python function takes a singly linked list of integers as parameter and rearranges the elements of the list. The function is called with the list containing the integers 78, 60, 77, 30, 42, 74, 66, 50 in the given order. What will be the contents of the list after the function completes execution?</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/11/113a094961fe5dde219d.png\" /></div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"77, 60, 78, 42, 74, 30, 66, 50"},{"key":"B","html":"60, 78, 30, 77, 74, 42, 50, 66"},{"key":"C","html":"77, 60, 78, 42, 30, 66, 74, 50"},{"key":"D","html":"77, 60, 42, 30, 66, 74, 78, 50"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>"},{"n":21,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p><em>X</em> is a continuous random variable with probability density function given by</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/da/daa525bbb27e6496b78a.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Find <em>k</em> and mean value of <em>X</em>.</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e9/e99bba8adaebb8db325b.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3e/3ee73a575ddc2355f10a.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ce/ced9dc9e33ab37c6258e.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/59/59cd1da9f2d0b6436586.png\" />"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/bc/bc7776ede5b6e2dc1505.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/13/1399bf6013686838c38b.png\" /></div>"},{"n":22,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following recurrence relation:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/57/579eab14f237a93490d0.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Solve the above recurrence relation asymptotically and select the correct option:</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/60/6020c2e3aa5829152d29.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/19/198ccad5aca9d37f2e27.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e7/e73cfd231ce38f5546b3.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/f3/f394a69aebcb6db48b19.png\" />"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ef/ef0519874916ab3ab516.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ac/acb73f2da257976f89d1.png\" /></div>"},{"n":23,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider we toss a coin repeatedly. The coin is unfair and <em>P</em>(<em>H</em>) = <em>p</em> and <em>P</em>(<em>T</em>) = <em>q</em>. The game ends the first time that two consecutive heads (<em>HH</em>) or two consecutive tails (<em>TT</em>) are observed. We win if <em>HH</em> is observed and lose if TT is observed. For example if the outcome is <em>HTHTT</em> we lose. On the other hand, if the outcome is <em>THTHTHH</em>, we win.<br />\nWhat is the probability that we win?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/22/226ba216bb62ef5c4f6a.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/32/32e4b717c38103966008.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/8c/8ceeb0b9ed603943dafa.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3b/3b47d51139c0ffc238e2.png\" />"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/01/01abb04ec859a59ef163.png\" /></div>"},{"n":24,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider a linear SVM with parameters:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ce/ce76beacb355fd8715fd.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Which of the following statements are correct? (Select all that apply)</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>f</em>(<em>A</em>) = 3"},{"key":"B","html":"<em>f</em>(<em>A</em>) > 0 → point <em>A</em> belongs to class +1"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>f</em>(<em>B</em>) = 5"},{"key":"D","html":"<em>f</em>(<em>B</em>) < 0 → point <em>B</em> belongs to class –1"}],"answer":["A","B","C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, b, c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/bb/bb96f52c8b20863b26b7.png\" /></div>"},{"n":25,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following statements:<br />\n<strong><em>S</em><sub>1</sub> :</strong> The function prints the binary representation of n by first pushing remainders into a stack and then popping them to display digits in the correct order.<br />\n<strong><em>S</em><sub>2</sub> :</strong> The function prints the binary representation of n in reverse order, since remainders are generated from least significant bit to most significant bit.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>1</sub>"},{"key":"B","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"C","html":"Both <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> nor <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\n&bull; The first while loop divides n repeatedly by 2 and pushes remainders into the stack.<br />\n&bull; These remainders are binary digits in reverse order.<br />\n&bull; The second loop pops from the stack, reversing them back to the correct order.<br />\nHence, the function prints the binary representation of n.</p>"},{"n":26,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following statements:<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : Linear probing is equivalent to double hashing with a secondary hash function of <em>h</em><sub>2</sub>(<em>k</em>) = 1.<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : Linear probing can lead to primary clustering, where consecutive filled slots increase the chance of further collisions.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>1</sub>"},{"key":"B","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"C","html":"Both <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> nor <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/af/af0a94ffdaae1de88aaa.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":27,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Find the minimum number of tables required for converting the following ER diagram into relational database?</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/9c/9caa50e89ef4bc0f905e.png\" /></div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[4,4],"answerText":"4","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>4<br />\n&bull; <em>R</em><sub>12</sub> is one to many relationship <em>R</em><sub>23</sub> is one-one.<br />\n&bull; <em>R</em><sub>1</sub>&nbsp;has multivalued attribute, so it will split into two tables.<br />\nTables required are as follows:<br />\n(i) <em>R</em><sub>12</sub> <em>R</em><sub>2</sub><br />\n(ii) <em>R</em><sub>1</sub>&nbsp;(AB)<br />\n(iii)<em>R</em><sub>1</sub>&nbsp;(AC)<br />\n(iv)<em>R</em><sub>23</sub><em>R</em><sub>3</sub>&nbsp;(FG)<br />\nSo, total 4 tables required.</p>"},{"n":28,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Which of the following is/are correct?<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> : For all asymptotically positive <em>f</em>(<em>n</em>), <em>f</em>(<em>n</em>) + Ο( <em>f</em>(<em>n</em>)) = θ( <em>f</em>(<em>n</em>)).<br />\n<em>S</em><sub>2</sub> : The worst case running time and expected running time are equal to with in constant factors for any randomized algorithm.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>1</sub>"},{"key":"B","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"C","html":"Both <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> nor <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/96/9688f1d7e255b5149c34.png\" /></div>"},{"n":29,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>If <em>X</em><em> </em>is a continuous random variables whose probability density function is given by</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/cd/cde07419dc12bae446dc.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>The value of ‘<em>p</em>’ is</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b2/b2102d3f4bc905c3b58c.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2a/2aab3600bba0f1e931b5.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/fb/fb4b78fbe2639c41d180.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/04/0426818ca8a8870fb67a.png\" />"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/09/094d79b01e980cfd4170.png\" /></div>"},{"n":30,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider a relation <em>R</em>(<em>a</em>, <em>b</em>, <em>c</em>, <em>d</em>, <em>e</em>) containing 500000 records, where each data page holds 100 records.<br />\n<em>R</em> is organized as a sorted files on candidate key. Assume R.a is a candidate key for <em>R</em>, with values lying in the range 0 to 4, 999, 99.<br />\nGiven the following relational algebra queries.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/82/82f08d29b39e9a2f00bd.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Which of the following is a false statement?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Linear hashed index is most efficient for <em>Q</em><sub>2</sub>, but inefficient for <em>Q</em>1 and <em>Q</em><sub>3</sub>."},{"key":"B","html":"A clustered <em>B</em><sup>+</sup> tree is optimal for both <em>Q</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>Q</em><sub>3</sub> but not useful for <em>Q</em><sub>2</sub> and <em>Q</em><sub>4</sub>."},{"key":"C","html":"Full scan is optimal for <em>Q</em><sub>4</sub> but not for <em>Q</em><sub>1</sub>."},{"key":"D","html":"Hashing is preferable to clustering for <em>Q</em><sub>3</sub> but inefficient for <em>Q</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>Q</em><sub>4</sub>."}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e7/e7310356ba859bda6d0a.png\" /></div>"},{"n":31,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0","html":"<p>A dataset consists of 120000 samples. To select the optimal value of a single hyperparameter from 5 possible choices, a researcher applies leave-one-out (LOO) cross-validation and 5-fold crossvalidation. Determine how many times greater the total number of models trained in LOO crossvalidation is compared to 5-fold cross-validation.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[24000,24000],"answerText":"24000","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>24000</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a6/a6495788ac95de7e46c4.png\" /></div>"},{"n":32,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following algorithms is/are complete?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Depth-Limited Search (DLS)"},{"key":"B","html":"Breadth-First Search (BFS)"},{"key":"C","html":"Greedy Best-First Search (GBFS)"},{"key":"D","html":"Iterative Deepening Depth-First Search (IDDFS)"}],"answer":["C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C, D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b, d)</p>"},{"n":33,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Which of the following statements is correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Singular values are always non-negative."},{"key":"B","html":"Singular vectors are always unique."},{"key":"C","html":"A zero matrix has all singular values equal to 1."},{"key":"D","html":"Rank of a matrix equals the number of zero singular values."}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>"},{"n":34,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>A dataset of 80 samples, with 40 samples of class X and 40 samples of class Y, is split by feature F in two ways:<br />\n<strong>Split 1:</strong><br />\n• Left has 30 samples, which include 18 samples of class X and the remaining from class Y.<br />\n• Right has 50 samples, which include 22 samples of class X and the remaining from class Y.<br />\n<strong>Split 2:</strong><br />\n• Left has 40 samples, which include 20 samples of class X and the remaining from class Y.<br />\n• Right has 40 samples, which include 20 samples of class X and the remaining from class Y.<br />\nWhich of the following is correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Difference between weighted Gini index is 0.012."},{"key":"B","html":"Difference between weighted Gini index is 0.256."},{"key":"C","html":"Gini index of left in the split 2 is 0.32."},{"key":"D","html":"Gini index right and left of split 2 both are same."}],"answer":["A","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/15/15e053ac2eb952c81510.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c6/c610c4bc67a2c761db47.png\" /></div>"},{"n":35,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Following is Python code of a function that takes a number as an argument, and uses a stack (list) to do processing.</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/19/192793c5aa0d35812515.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Which of the following is correct?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Prints binary representation of <em>n</em> in reverse order."},{"key":"B","html":"Prints binary representation of <em>n</em>."},{"key":"C","html":"Prints the value of log <em>n</em>."},{"key":"D","html":"Prints the value of log <em>n</em> in reverse order."}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nThe first while loop repeatedly divides n by 2 and pushes the remainders (<em>n</em> % 2) into the stack.<br />\nThese remainders represent binary digits of n but in reverse order.<br />\nThe second loop pops elements from the stack, which reverses them back to the correct order.</p>"},{"n":36,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0","html":"<p>A dataset has values <em>X</em> = {15, 20, 25, 30, 35}, mean = 25, standard deviation (σ) = 5.<br />\nFind the z-score normalized value for x = 30.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[1,1],"answerText":"1","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>1</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/18/18468161e1b21bb6152c.png\" /></div>"},{"n":37,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>A random variable <em>X</em> is uniformly distributed over the interval [0, 1]. Define two events:<br />\n<strong><em>A</em> :</strong> The outcome falls within [0, 0.75].<br />\n<strong><em>B</em> :</strong> The outcome lies in the range [0.5, 1].<br />\nWhat is the probability of event B occurring given that event A has occurred?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b6/b6e7cedb725a458b036d.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/17/1798c702de7e32c678a9.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7e/7e1b172141922bc3810f.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"1"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/74/741751c5727224004569.png\" /></div>"},{"n":38,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Imagine you are using a k-Nearest Neighbor classifier on a dataset with lots of noise. You want your classifier to be less sensitive to the noise. Which of the following is likely to help and with what side effect?<br />\n<strong><em>S</em><sub>1</sub> :</strong> Decrease the value of <em>k</em> → Decrease in prediction time.<br />\n<strong><em>S</em><sub>2</sub> :</strong> Increase the value of <em>k</em> → Increase in prediction time.<br />\nWhich of the above statements is correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>1</sub>"},{"key":"B","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"C","html":"Both <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"D","html":"Neither <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> nor <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>"},{"n":39,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the given relational schema <em>R</em>(<em>W</em>, <em>X</em>, <em>Y</em>, <em>Z</em>) and functional dependencies:<br />\n<em>WX</em> → <em>Y</em>, <em>Y</em> → <em>Z</em>, <em>Z</em> → <em>W</em><br />\nFind the number of non-trivial FD’s such that right side of dependency should have 1 attribute that follow from the given FD’s are ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[11,11],"answerText":"11","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>11</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/29/298eb4e9d2a8ee66f0a1.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ba/ba0dc2d3a56ca6ec0959.png\" /></div>"},{"n":40,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following functions:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/1a/1a8743a770217f87fadf.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Choose the correct option which ranks all functions in increasing order.</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"3 < 1 < 2 < 5 < 4"},{"key":"B","html":"3 < 2 < 1 < 4 < 5"},{"key":"C","html":"3 < 1 < 2 < 4 < 5"},{"key":"D","html":"3 < 2 < 1 < 5 < 4"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/57/576067e7525f0c1b3b53.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":41,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following statements :</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/1f/1ff47918b5ca0ebbac2a.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Which of the above is a tautology.</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only I"},{"key":"B","html":"Only II"},{"key":"C","html":"Both I and II"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/0d/0d538290640663212f40.png\" /></div>"},{"n":42,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the function:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/de/dee12330931e7acfc3c0.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>On which of the following intervals is f(x) increasing and concave down?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"(–2, 0)"},{"key":"B","html":"(0, 1)"},{"key":"C","html":"(1, 2)"},{"key":"D","html":"(–∞, –1)"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/4e/4e59daaef122dc65e029.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ba/ba1a52056e483b9972d7.png\" /></div>"},{"n":43,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following techniques can help prevent overfitting in a machine learning model?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Increasing the number of features without regularization"},{"key":"B","html":"Using Lasso or Ridge regression"},{"key":"C","html":"Reducing the size of the training dataset"},{"key":"D","html":"Using dropout in a neural network"}],"answer":["B","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(b, d)<br />\n&bull; Adding more features without regularization increases model complexity. This makes the<br />\nmodel fit noise in the data, causing overfitting. So, option (a) is incorrect.<br />\n&bull; Lasso (L1) and Ridge (L2) regression add penalties on large coefficients. This reduces model<br />\ncomplexity and prevents overfitting. So, option (b) is correct.<br />\n&bull; Reducing the dataset size decreases the amount of information for learning. Less data usually<br />\nworsens overfitting. So, option (c) is incorrect.<br />\n&bull; Dropout randomly ignores neurons during training in neural networks. This prevents the<br />\nnetwork from relying too much on any feature and improves generalization. So, option (d) is<br />\ncorrect.</p>"},{"n":44,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p><img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/57/574c25fc008ae06d0f75.png\" /></p>\n\n<p>Which of the following is correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<em>f</em>(3) is not distinct"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a3/a372ee6cb0f9e8088b35.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<em>f</em>(3) ≠ –5"},{"key":"D","html":"None of these"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/12/1259d0848315010cb3eb.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/4e/4e6c40b3b39d62e37b53.png\" /></div>"},{"n":45,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the adjacency list representation of graph, each vertex maintain list for outgoing edges only. Let <em>n</em> and <em>m</em> denote the number of vertices and edges respectively in graph and <em>k</em> is the maximum in-degree of a vertex.<br />\nWorst case to compute the in-degree of a given vertex?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/c5/c51f855a7e6e412b3f73.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/01/0188be880d8cc2649b2e.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/25/253c5a77a13f347e1bc8.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/7e/7e944b9f28e05b1c1d34.png\" />"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nWithout explicitly maintaining a list of incoming edges, you might have to scan all the edges to identify the incoming arcs.</p>"},{"n":46,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Two random variable W and Z are defined in two other zero mean random variable <em>X</em> and <em>Y</em> as given below:<br />\n<em>W</em> = <em>X</em> + <em>aY</em> and Z = <em>X</em> – <em>aY</em>; where “<em>a</em>” is a positive real constant.<br />\nIf the variance of “X” is 16 and the variance of “<em>Y</em>” is 4, then the value of “a”, that makes W and<br />\nZ orthogonal to each other, is equal to</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/9d/9d48dbad6d69c764b707.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"3"},{"key":"C","html":"2"},{"key":"D","html":"4"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ad/adfec93c15ae541bb7e2.png\" /></div>"},{"n":47,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Consider the following first order logic statements, where <em>P</em>(<em>n</em>) and <em>Q</em>(<em>n</em>) are predicates, which of the following statements is/are true?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/94/94d8ba660b4bec6618d6.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/5c/5c0410ea2577eaa051c2.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/fa/fa163229e69836511c1d.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/54/54a58dee0f88bb953232.png\" />"}],"answer":["A","B","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, B, C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, b, c, d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/e0/e07de38cb3b2d2daf7c8.png\" /></div>"},{"n":48,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following Python program segment where Node structure represents a node in a binary search tree:</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/97/9735539282dc6bb9f695.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>The value returned by BST when a reference to the root of a binary search tree is passed as its argument is:</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Sum of all nodes"},{"key":"B","html":"Number of leaf nodes"},{"key":"C","html":"Sum of internal nodes"},{"key":"D","html":"Sum of leaf nodes"}],"answer":["D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(d)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/eb/ebf3e64d2ee4880b448d.png\" /></div>"},{"n":49,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>The maximum value of the function <em>f</em> (<em>x</em>) = 2<em>x</em><sup>3</sup> – 15<em>x</em><sup>2</sup> + 36<em>x</em> + 2 in the interval [1, 5] will be equal to</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[57,57],"answerText":"57","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>57 [57 - 57]</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/65/651489e3688a07108b42.png\" /></div>"},{"n":50,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Consider the following:<br />\nInorder: 7, 14, 5, 2, 1, 9, 3, 6, 8, 4<br />\nPreorder: 1, 5, 7, 14, 2, 4, 3, 9, 8, 6<br />\nFind the number of elements in 4<sup>th</sup>, 5<sup>th</sup> level respectively.<br />\nAssume root is at level.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"3, 3"},{"key":"B","html":"3, 1"},{"key":"C","html":"3, 2"},{"key":"D","html":"2, 3"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/80/80531bb671c9fb230e66.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":51,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Let <em>A</em><sub><em>n</em> × <em>n</em></sub> be matrix of order <em>n</em> and <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/9d/9ddd3a509100cf468988.png\" /> be the matrix obtained by interchanging the first and second rows of unit matrix <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/f8/f8104034905513d859ab.png\" /> is such that its first</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"row is the same as its second row"},{"key":"B","html":"row is the same as second row of A"},{"key":"C","html":"column is the same as the second column of A"},{"key":"D","html":"row is a zero row"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/dd/ddd5598bcb61ed6c1397.png\" /></div>"},{"n":52,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider a Bayesian network with multiple variables. Gibbs sampling is applied to estimate the<br />\njoint distribution. Which of the following statements is correct?<br />\n<strong><em>S</em><sub>1</sub> :</strong> Gibbs sampling converges to the true joint distribution after infinite iterations.<br />\n<strong><em>S</em><sub>2</sub> :</strong> Gibbs sampling cannot handle discrete variables.<br />\n<strong>S<sub>3</sub> :</strong> The method is guaranteed to produce exact samples in a finite number of steps.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>1</sub>"},{"key":"B","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"C","html":"Only <em>S</em><sub>3</sub>"},{"key":"D","html":"<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub>"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)<br />\n&bull; Gibbs sampling is a Markov Chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) method. As the number of iterations &rarr; &infin;, the samples converge to the target joint distribution. So, <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> is correct.<br />\n&bull; Gibbs sampling works for both discrete and continuous variables. There is no restriction on the type of variables as long as we can sample from the conditional distributions. So, <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> is incorrect.<br />\n&bull; Gibbs sampling produces approximate samples, not exact ones, in a finite number of steps. Exact convergence requires infinite iteration. So, <em>S</em><sub>3</sub> is incorrect.</p>"},{"n":53,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following distance matrix of 4 points <em>X</em>, <em>Y</em>, <em>Z</em>, <em>W</em> :</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/3d/3ddcfc42b65b9316a16d.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>Use Agglomerative Hierarchical Clustering with Single Linkage to form clusters step-by-step.<br />\nShow the order of merges.</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/6c/6c1c27c5b6404e3d35c2.png\" />"},{"key":"B","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/d4/d447cd2c77fce43ad6ac.png\" />"},{"key":"C","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2a/2aa1ef8a4539f4b5b8d4.png\" />"},{"key":"D","html":"<img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a5/a573006bcf73de365492.png\" />"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/a8/a87cf137ffd3aba1cf51.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/09/09986fbc48f997e3bf85.png\" /></div>"},{"n":54,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider following 2 queries with relation schema :</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/71/7144e4677797afefb954.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>Which of the following query results “all students who never got a grade above 4.0”?</div>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Only <em>Q</em><sub>1</sub>"},{"key":"B","html":"Only <em>Q</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"C","html":"Both <em>Q</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>Q</em><sub>2</sub>"},{"key":"D","html":"None of them"}],"answer":["C"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"C","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(c)<br />\n&bull; Query 1 uses a correlated subquery with NOT EXISTS, implementing an anti-join to exclude students having any course with Grade &gt; 4.0. It also includes student with no enrollment records. Since, no matching tuple exist in course.<br />\n&bull; Query 2 performs a set difference between all student.ID S and those in course with grade &gt; 4.0,<br />\nfollowed by a join with student to get complete records. This also retains student who never enrolled in any course.<br />\nSo, both queries return the same result.</p>"},{"n":55,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following statements is/are correct?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"PCA reduces the dimensionality of a dataset by projecting it into a smaller feature space."},{"key":"B","html":"PCA completely eliminates multicollinearity in the dataset."},{"key":"C","html":"PCA can reduce overfitting."},{"key":"D","html":"Multicollinearity occurs when two or more independent variables are highly correlated with each other."}],"answer":["A","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, C, D","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, c, d)<br />\n&bull; PCA reduces dimensionality by projecting data onto principal components. This keeps most of the variance while simplifying the dataset. So, option (a) is correct.<br />\n&bull; PCA reduces correlation among variables but does not completely eliminate multicollinearity<br />\nin all cases. So, option (b) is incorrect.<br />\n&bull; By reducing dimensionality and removing redundant information, PCA can help decrease<br />\noverfitting in predictive models. So, option (c) is correct.<br />\n&bull; Multicollinearity occurs when independent variables are highly correlated, which can cause<br />\ninstability in model coefficient estimates. So, option (d) is correct.</p>"},{"n":56,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the sorted array:<br />\n[12, 18, 24, 30, 36, 42, 48, 54, 60, 66, 72]<br />\nIf you are searching for the number 33 using binary search, how many comparisons are required before determining that 33 is not in  he list?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"3"},{"key":"B","html":"4"},{"key":"C","html":"5"},{"key":"D","html":"6"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)<br />\nBinary search compares with the middle element.<br />\nArray length 11 middle index = 5 &rarr; (0-based) &rarr; middle element = 42<br />\nCompare 33 &lt; 42 &rarr; go to left half [12, 18, 24, 30, 36] comparisons = 1<br />\nLeft half length = 5 &rarr; middle index = 2 &rarr; element = 24<br />\nCompare 33 &gt; 24 &rarr; go to right half [30, 36] comparisons = 2<br />\nRight half length = 2 &rarr; middle index = 0 &rarr; element = 30<br />\nCompare 33 &gt; 30 &rarr; go to right half [36] comparisons = 3<br />\nOnly one element left &rarr; element = 36<br />\nCompare 33 &lt; 36 &rarr; go to left half [ ] comparisons = 4</p>"},{"n":57,"type":"MSQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Which of the following statement(s) is/are true?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Quicksort and merge sort are both examples of divide and conquer algorithms."},{"key":"B","html":"If we randomly choose a pivot element each time, quicksort will always terminate in time Ο(<em>n</em> log <em>n</em>)."},{"key":"C","html":"For every fixed strategy to choose a pivot for quicksort, we can construct a worst case input that requires time Ο(<em>n</em>)."},{"key":"D","html":"If we could find the median in time Ο(1), quicksort would have worst case complexity Ο(<em>n</em> log <em>n</em>)."}],"answer":["A","C","D"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A, C, D","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>(a, c, d)<br />\nOption (a) both Quick Sort and Merge Sort are both examples of divide and conquer. In Merge<br />\nSort we do blind division and try to conquer on the small sub problems while in Quick Sort we<br />\nperform meaning division so we don&rsquo;t bear the conquer cost.<br />\nOption (b) if we randomly choose a pivot the quick sort will not end up giving &theta;(n log n) time<br />\ncomplexity in every case but less no of groups will the worst case of &theta;(n2), for sure.<br />\nOption (c) every partition technique takes &Omicron;(n) to choose the pivot whether its Hoare or Lomuto<br />\nanalysis of Hoare vs Lomuto:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Quicksort<br />\nOption (d) given that we can find the median in &Omicron;(1) we can always simulate Quick Sort to<br />\nperform in &theta;(n log n) but generally the median finding takes more time and Merge Sort out<br />\nperforms the constant factor that gets added for median using the median of medians finding<br />\nand then applying the Quick Sort.<br />\nCorrect answers will be option (a), (c) and (d).</p>"},{"n":58,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0.33","html":"<p>Find the eigen values of the matrix <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/e7/e77af5f248290c698c63.png\" /></p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"[2, 3, 4]"},{"key":"B","html":"[2, 3, 5]"},{"key":"C","html":"[1, 4, 5]"},{"key":"D","html":"[1, 3, 5]"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0.33},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/b1/b1300d0f07fa37dcbcfa.png\" /></div>"},{"n":59,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>The value of <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/87/876788537e194cbdc676.png\" /> is ________.</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[-0.25,-0.2],"answerText":"-0.22","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>-0.22 (-0.25 - -0.20)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/aa/aa8bf5cdb17bf775610b.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":60,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Consider the following statement <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> about the relational data model:<br />\n<strong><em>S</em><sub>1</sub>:</strong> A relation scheme can have at most one foreign key.<br />\n<strong><em>S</em><sub>2</sub>:</strong> A foreign key in a relation scheme R cannot be used to refer to tuples of <em>R</em>.</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"Both <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> are true"},{"key":"B","html":"Both <em>S</em><sub>1</sub> and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> are false"},{"key":"C","html":"S1 is false and S2 is true"},{"key":"D","html":"<em>S</em><sub>1</sub> is true and <em>S</em><sub>2</sub> is false"}],"answer":["B"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"B","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(b)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/2f/2f419bac266dd7c0a528.png\" /></div>"},{"n":61,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>A hash table of size 10 using open addressing with linear probing and hash function is <em>h</em>(<em>k</em>) = (<em>k</em>) mod 10, where k is key value. Initially table is empty. Following keys are inserted into table in given order:<br />\n64, 85, 43, 14, 87, 30, 10<br />\nHow many number of probes required to insert 13 in table after inserting above keys?</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[6,6],"answerText":"6","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>6</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/4a/4a6e30a276febfd95930.png\" /></div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/10/1045594d441a00c9521a.png\" /></div>"},{"n":62,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p><em>X</em> and <em>Y</em> are two identical and independent random variables, each is uniformly distributed in the interval [–2, 2]. The probability that min [<em>X</em>, <em>Y</em>] is less than <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/ea/eaf1bbbafda4ce813f9f.png\" /> is ________. (Upto 2 decimal places)</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[0.8,0.9],"answerText":"0.85","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>0.85 [0.80 - 0.90]</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/4c/4c4ab83607b59bb81ebb.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":63,"type":"MCQ","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0.66","html":"<p>Let <em>g</em>(<em>x</em>) be continuous on [2, 6] and differentiable on (2, 6). Suppose that <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/49/49b4260b494ddc92db3b.png\" /> and <em>g</em>(2) = 3. Based on the Mean Value Theorem (MVT).<br />\nWhich of the following statements must hold true for the value of g(6)?</p>","options":[{"key":"A","html":"g(6) > 19"},{"key":"B","html":"g(6) < 19"},{"key":"C","html":"g(6) = 19"},{"key":"D","html":"g(6) > 17"}],"answer":["A"],"natRange":null,"answerText":"A","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0.66},"solution":"<p>(a)</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/93/93de5ab67475f446358b.png\" /></div>"},{"n":64,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +2 / -0","html":"<p>The joint probability density of random variable <em>X</em> and <em>Y</em> given as</p>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/1f/1f748650178d9e0dead3.png\" /></div>\n\n<div> </div>\n\n<div>The probability that <em>X</em> ≤ 0, <em>Y</em> ≤ 1 is <img align=\"absmiddle\" alt=\"\" src=\"media/a7/a77fa42b61736b996248.png\" /> Then the value of Z is _________.</div>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[1,1],"answerText":"1","marks":{"pos":2,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>1</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/ff/ff45573755f9d3521a4c.png\" /></div>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>"},{"n":65,"type":"NAT","section":"Technical","tags":"Technical • Data Science & Artificial Intelligence • +1 / -0","html":"<p>Let <em>X</em><sub>1</sub>, <em>X</em><sub>2</sub>, <em>X</em><sub>3</sub> be pairwise uncorrelated random variables with <em>E</em>[<em>X</em><sub>i</sub>] = 0 and Var(<em>X<sub>i</sub></em>) = 2 for all <em>i</em>.<br />\nFind the value of Cov(<em>X</em><sub>1</sub> + 2<em>X</em><sub>2</sub>, 3<em>X</em><sub>2</sub> – <em>X</em><sub>3</sub>) is ________?</p>","options":[],"answer":null,"natRange":[12,12],"answerText":"12","marks":{"pos":1,"neg":0},"solution":"<p>12</p>\n\n<div>&nbsp;</div>\n\n<div><img alt=\"\" src=\"media/4f/4f4106d6d233b439f997.png\" /></div>"}],"math":false,"scoring":"marks","sections":[{"name":"General Aptitude","start":0,"count":10},{"name":"Technical","start":10,"count":55}],"stats":{"questions":65,"maxMarks":100,"types":{"MCQ":45,"MSQ":8,"NAT":12},"withoutKey":0}};
